From: NeiroNext <mihail.rozshko@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 22:16:35 +0000
Subject: Export SessionIsActive on org.gnome.SessionManager

gnome-session exports SessionIsActive since 3.7.2 (logind's Session.Active
of the session it runs), and gnome-settings-daemon's plugins of that era
read it: the automount helper mounts nothing while it is absent, the color
plugin logs "assertion 'active_v != NULL' failed" on every property change
and the power plugin never enables its idle handling. cinnamon-session
never had the property, so a Unity session on cinnamon-session (Ubuntu
Unity 26.04, unity-settings-daemon) got none of the three.

CsmSystem gets an "active" property (default TRUE: a session that cannot
be found must not look inactive). The logind backend implements it with a
proxy on our session, reading Active and following its changes (a VT
switch, fast user switching). Finding the session no longer relies on the
process being in a session scope: cinnamon-session started by a systemd
user service (unity-session.service) is not, and sd_pid_get_session fails
with "Could not get session id for session" on every login. The
candidates are the process's own session, XDG_SESSION_ID, and logind's
User.Display, each verified with GetSession; this also gives the existing
users of the session path (SetIdleHint, CanSwitchUser) a session. The
manager binds the property to the exported interface right after the
export, SYNC_CREATE, so that a client created right afterwards reads the
right value.

The ConsoleKit backend already carried an override of "active"; it now
refers to a real property.

Assisted-by: LLM claude-fable-5-1
---
 cinnamon-session/csm-manager.c                |   8 +
 cinnamon-session/csm-system.c                 |  12 ++
 cinnamon-session/csm-systemd.c                | 211 ++++++++++++++++++++++++--
